# The Delhi Sultanate: Five Dynasties, 320 Years

> Between 1206 and 1526 five dynasties ruled from Delhi — Mamluk, Khalji,
> Tughlaq, Sayyid, and Lodi — building an Indo-Persian state on the iqta
> system, a horse-mounted army, and the tanka and jital coinage, while
> Sufi khanqahs and Bhakti saints reshaped popular religion.

## 1. The Turkish Onslaught

**Mahmud of Ghazni** (971-1030) raided India seventeen times between 1000
and 1027 — the sack of **Somnath (1025)** the most notorious — but took
plunder, not territory. His court housed **Firdausi** (*Shahnama*) and
**Alberuni** (*Kitab-ul-Hind*).

| Battle | Year | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First Tarain | 1191 | Prithviraj Chauhan III defeats Ghori |
| Second Tarain | 1192 | Ghori's mounted archers destroy Prithviraj |
| Chandawar | 1194 | Jaichandra of Kanauj crushed |

Ghori returned to Ghazni, leaving his slave-general **Qutb-ud-din Aibak**
in charge; after Ghori's assassination in 1206, Aibak founded the
Sultanate. **Bakhtiyar Khalji** had already swept Bengal (Nadiya, 1203).

