## 3. Mamluks: Making the State (1206-1290)

- **Qutb-ud-din Aibak** (1206-1210), "Lakh Baksh" (giver of lakhs), began
  the **Qutub Minar** and died of a polo accident.
- **Iltutmish** (1211-1236), the true architect: regularised the **iqta**,
  finished the Qutub Minar, minted the silver **tanka** and copper
  **jital**, organised the **Chahalgani** ("Forty"), and saved India when
  **Chengiz Khan's** hordes reached the Indus (1221).
- **Razia** (1236-1240), the only woman on Delhi's throne, fell to Turkish
  noble resentment in four years.
- **Balban** (1266-1287) ruled by "blood and iron": shattered the
  Chahalgani, called himself **Zil-i-Ilahi** (shadow of God), ran the
  *barid* spies, and built the **Diwan-i-Arz** (military department).

## 4. The Khalji Revolution (1290-1320)

**Alauddin Khalji** (1296-1316) seized the throne after raiding wealthy
**Devagiri**.

| Field | Measure |
| --- | --- |
| Conquest | Gujarat 1299, Ranthambhore 1301, Chittor 1303, Malwa 1305 |
| Deccan | Malik Kafur's raids on Warangal and Dwarasamudra, 1308-1313 |
| Mongol defence | Victories at Jalandhar, Kili (1299), Amroha, and the Ravi |
| Army | First permanent standing army; **dagh** branding, **chehra** muster |
| Revenue | Land measured; demand fixed at 50% of produce |

[important title="The market experiment"]
Alauddin fixed prices in Delhi through the **Diwan-i-Riyasat** with the
**shahna-i-mandi** enforcing separate markets for grain, cloth, and horses
— history's earliest detailed price-control regime, aimed at paying a
large army cheaply.
[/important]

## 5. The Tughlaq Experiments (1320-1414)

**Muhammad bin Tughlaq** (1325-1351), the "wisest fool," attempted four
bold failures: shifting the capital from Delhi to **Daulatabad** (1327,
reversed 1335); **token copper currency** (1329) valued as silver, sunk by
counterfeiting; raising an army for a **Khorasan** invasion that never
sailed; and the **Qarachil** expedition into the Himalayas, destroyed. His
**Diwan-i-Amir Kohi** for agriculture and the Deccan's slip into the new
**Bahmani kingdom (1347)** completed the mix of genius and misjudgement —
recorded eyewitness by the Moroccan **Ibn Battuta**, his qazi of Delhi
(1333-1342).

**Firuz Shah Tughlaq** (1351-1388) answered chaos with welfare: canals
from the Yamuna and Sutlej, new towns, free hospitals (*dar-ul-shafa*),
translations of Sanskrit medical works, and a slave department of 180,000.
After him ruin was quick — **Timur sacked Delhi in 1398** — and the
dynasty ended in 1414.

## 6. Sayyids and Lodis (1414-1526)

The **Sayyids** (Khizr Khan and successors) held a shrunken Delhi under
Timur's overlordship. The Afghan **Lodis** followed: Bahlul consolidated,
**Sikandar Lodi** founded **Agra (1504)** and annexed Gwalior, and
**Ibrahim Lodi's** arrogance drove his nobles and Rana Sanga to invite
**Babur**, who destroyed the Lodi army at the **First Battle of Panipat,
21 April 1526**.

## 7. Administration and the Iqta System

| Institution | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Iqta | Revenue assignment to a muqti for salary and troops |
| Khalisa | Land whose revenue went directly to the crown |
| Diwan-i-Wizarat | Finance under the wazir |
| Diwan-i-Arz | Military recruitment and pay |
| Diwan-i-Insha | Royal correspondence |
| Diwan-i-Risalat | Religious affairs and petitions |

[definition]
An **iqta** was a territorial assignment: its holder collected the land
revenue, kept a fixed salary from it, and maintained cavalry for the
Sultan. Transferable and non-hereditary in theory, it was the Sultanate's
fiscal-military backbone — the base Alauddin tightened with measurement
and cash salaries.
[/definition]

## 8. Sufis, Saints, and Southern Contemporaries

| Sufi order | Centre | Key figures |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chishti | Ajmer, Delhi | Muinuddin Chishti (d. 1236), Qutbuddin Bakhtiar Kaki, Nizamuddin Auliya (d. 1325) |
| Suhrawardi | Multan | Bahauddin Zakariya |
| Firdausi | Bihar, Badayun | Suhrawardi offshoot |

The Chishtis lived simply, refused state grants, and used **sama**
(devotional music — Amir Khusrau's verses); **Nizamuddin Auliya** of Delhi
was the greatest. In parallel the Bhakti current rose — **Namadeva and
Tukaram** (Maharashtra), **Ramananda** and his disciple **Kabir**
(Varanasi), **Guru Nanak** (b. 1469), **Mirabai**, and **Chaitanya** in
Bengal — preaching in vernacular tongues. In the Deccan,
**Vijayanagara** (founded 1336 by Harihara and Bukka) faced the **Bahmani
kingdom** (1347, Gulbarga then Bidar); under **Krishnadevaraya**
(1509-1529) Hampi reached its peak before the disaster of **Talikota
(1565)**.

## Key Takeaways

- Ghori's victory at Second Tarain (1192) opened north India to Turkish
  rule; Aibak converted conquest into a state in 1206.
- Iltutmish built its institutions (iqta, tanka, Chahalgani); Balban its
  ideology; Alauddin its army, revenue, and price system.
- Muhammad bin Tughlaq's experiments and Firuz Shah's welfare mark the
  Tughlaq paradox; Timur's 1398 sack exposed the decay.
- Persian statecraft, Sufi piety, and Indo-Islamic art formed the
  inheritance the Mughals took over after Panipat, 1526.
